</figure>
<div id="A1.SS11.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS11.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ A.11 Variance and Failure Analysis ‣ Appendix A Technical Appendix ‣ TRACE: Trajectory-Routed Causal Memory for Delayed-Evidence Visuomotor Imitation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> gives the failure categories used to interpret the high Tool variance. The categories separate whether TRACE reaches the delayed branch with the correct object-conditioned decision from later contact-rich losses. This distinction matters because Tool can produce mid-range or low progress for different reasons. Some rollouts preserve the delayed cue but lose downstream stages through contact, while others fail the memory-dependent branch itself.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T18" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 18: </span>Tool failure taxonomy for interpreting the high-variance rollouts. The categories separate delayed-memory failures from contact and recovery failures after the branch.</figcaption>
<div id="A1.T18.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:392.2pt;vertical-align:-193.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(4.0pt,-4.6pt) scale(1.02375671868193,1.02375671868193) ;">
<table id="A1.T18.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
